"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[24761],{924761:e=>{e.exports="# Semi-Structured Data — VARIANT / OBJECT / ARRAY\n\nSnowflake's signature semi-structured surface — `VARIANT`, `OBJECT`, `ARRAY`, plus the construction, path-access, and conversion functions that go with them — and how each maps to DBSQL.\n\n## SNOWFLAKE\n\n### Type mapping\n\n| Snowflake | DBSQL                                                            | Notes                                                                                                                              |\n| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `VARIANT` | `VARIANT` (DBR 15.3+) **or** `STRING` (JSON)                     | DBSQL has native `VARIANT` from DBR 15.3+. On older runtimes use `STRING` and parse with `from_json` or `get_json_object`.         |\n| `OBJECT`  | `MAP<STRING, VARIANT>` **or** `STRUCT<...>`                      | Snowflake `OBJECT` is a VARIANT-typed map. Use DBSQL `MAP` when keys are dynamic; `STRUCT` when the shape is fixed.                |\n| `ARRAY`   | `ARRAY<VARIANT>` (heterogeneous) **or** `ARRAY<T>` (homogeneous) | Snowflake arrays are heterogeneous (VARIANT-valued); DBSQL arrays are homogeneous. `ARRAY<VARIANT>` preserves Snowflake semantics. |\n\n### VARIANT notes\n\nSnowflake `VARIANT` can hold any value — scalar, object, array. DBSQL `VARIANT` (DBR 15.3+) is compatible. On older DBR versions, store the data as `STRING` (JSON) and extract — the `:` path operator works on both `VARIANT` and `STRING`-with-JSON, so the query syntax is typically unchanged.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: extract nested field\nSELECT variant_col:address.city::STRING FROM t;\n\n-- DBSQL — same syntax; `variant_col` may be VARIANT (DBR 15.3+) or STRING-with-JSON\nSELECT variant_col:address.city::STRING FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t;\n\n-- DBSQL with an explicit schema (alternative when type inference is needed)\nSELECT\n  FROM_JSON(variant_col, 'STRUCT<address: STRUCT<city: STRING>>').address.city\nFROM my_catalog.my_schema.t;\n\n-- DBSQL: path-based extraction without schema\nSELECT get_json_object(variant_col, '$.address.city') FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t;\n```\n\n### OBJECT vs STRUCT vs MAP\n\nSnowflake `OBJECT` is an untyped key-value container holding VARIANT values. DBSQL has two closely related options:\n\n- **`STRUCT<field1: type1, field2: type2>`** — when the shape is known and fixed. Faster access, enforced schema.\n- **`MAP<STRING, VARIANT>`** — when keys are dynamic or the shape varies per row. More Snowflake-like.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE TABLE t (attrs OBJECT);\nINSERT INTO t VALUES (OBJECT_CONSTRUCT('name', 'Alice', 'age', 30));\n\n-- DBSQL (fixed schema — preferred)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t (attrs STRUCT<name: STRING, age: INT>);\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (NAMED_STRUCT('name', 'Alice', 'age', 30));\n\n-- DBSQL (dynamic keys — Snowflake-like)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t (attrs MAP<STRING, VARIANT>);\n```\n\n### ARRAY notes\n\nSnowflake arrays are heterogeneous (any element may be any type, wrapped as VARIANT). DBSQL arrays are homogeneous.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: mixed types are fine\nARRAY_CONSTRUCT(1, 'text', TRUE)   -- legal\n\n-- DBSQL homogeneous: upcast to a common type (usually STRING or VARIANT)\nARRAY(1, 'text', TRUE)     -- type-checked: will fail or upcast\nARRAY(CAST(1 AS VARIANT), CAST('text' AS VARIANT), CAST(TRUE AS VARIANT))   -- heterogeneous via VARIANT\n```\n\nWhen migrating a Snowflake array column that held mixed types, declare the DBSQL column as `ARRAY<VARIANT>` (DBR 15.3+). If the source array is known to be homogeneous, use the concrete type for better performance.\n\n### Path access (`:` and `::`)\n\nBoth dialects support `:` for path navigation and `::` for cast — substitution is mostly safe. DBSQL's `:` operator accepts either a native `VARIANT` column (DBR 15.3+) **or** a `STRING` column containing valid JSON, so most Snowflake source works unchanged on any recent runtime.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nSELECT data:name::STRING, data:address.city::STRING FROM t;\n\n-- DBSQL — same syntax; `data` may be VARIANT (DBR 15.3+) or STRING-with-JSON\nSELECT data:name::STRING, data:address.city::STRING FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t;\n\n-- DBSQL alternatives when you want an explicit schema or a path-string API\nSELECT\n  FROM_JSON(data, 'STRUCT<name: STRING, address: STRUCT<city: STRING>>').name,\n  get_json_object(data, '$.address.city')\nFROM my_catalog.my_schema.t;\n```\n\n### OBJECT_CONSTRUCT / ARRAY_CONSTRUCT\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: build an object (JSON-like)\nSELECT OBJECT_CONSTRUCT('name', name, 'age', age) FROM users;\n\n-- DBSQL: named struct (or to_json for string output)\nSELECT NAMED_STRUCT('name', name, 'age', age) FROM my_catalog.my_schema.users;\nSELECT TO_JSON(NAMED_STRUCT('name', name, 'age', age)) FROM my_catalog.my_schema.users;\n\n-- Snowflake: build an array\nSELECT ARRAY_CONSTRUCT(1, 2, 3);\n\n-- DBSQL\nSELECT ARRAY(1, 2, 3);\n```\n\n### FLATTEN — array and object expansion\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: flatten an array into rows\nSELECT value\nFROM my_table, LATERAL FLATTEN(INPUT => my_array_col);\n\n-- DBSQL: LATERAL VIEW EXPLODE\nSELECT value\nFROM my_catalog.my_schema.my_table\nLATERAL VIEW EXPLODE(my_array_col) AS value;\n\n-- DBSQL: inline explode (simpler when one row-set per call)\nSELECT EXPLODE(my_array_col) AS value FROM my_catalog.my_schema.my_table;\n\n-- Snowflake: flatten an object (returns KEY, VALUE pairs)\nSELECT KEY, VALUE\nFROM my_table, LATERAL FLATTEN(INPUT => my_object_col);\n\n-- DBSQL: explode on map_from_entries or on a MAP column\nSELECT KEY, VALUE\nFROM my_catalog.my_schema.my_table\nLATERAL VIEW EXPLODE(my_object_col) AS KEY, VALUE;\n-- For VARIANT/object: CAST(col AS MAP<STRING,VARIANT>) first, then EXPLODE\n```\n\n### Conversion functions\n\n| Snowflake            | DBSQL                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Notes                                                              |\n| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `TO_VARIANT(expr)`   | `CAST(expr AS VARIANT)` (DBR 15.3+); on older runtimes there is no direct equivalent — serialize complex values with `TO_JSON(expr)` and round-trip via `FROM_JSON` / `get_json_object`                        | Wrap a typed value as VARIANT                                      |\n| `PARSE_JSON(str)`    | `PARSE_JSON(str)` (DBR 15.3+) or `FROM_JSON(str, schema)`                                                                                                                                                      | `PARSE_JSON` returns VARIANT; `FROM_JSON` requires explicit schema |\n| `TO_JSON(variant)`   | `TO_JSON(variant)`                                                                                                                                                                                             | Same                                                               |\n| `TO_ARRAY(expr)`     | `ARRAY(expr)`                                                                                                                                                                                                  | Single-element array                                               |\n| `TO_OBJECT(variant)` | No direct cast — VARIANT cannot be cast to `MAP` in DBSQL. Project the known fields into a `STRUCT` (`SELECT NAMED_STRUCT('a', v:a, 'b', v:b) FROM t`) or extract via `:` path access on the VARIANT directly. | Snowflake's untyped object has no exact DBSQL equivalent           |\n"}}]);
